        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Optimum Nutrition or Muscle Milk Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands have powders in the NSF Certified for Sport directory, but Muscle Milk's coverage is wider. When we searched the NSF directory on 19 November 2026, it listed Muscle Milk Genuine protein powder in Chocolate, Vanilla Crème, Strawberries 'N Crème, Cookies 'N Crème and Banana Crème, and Pro Series in Knockout Chocolate, Intense Vanilla and Slammin' Strawberry, among other Muscle Milk products. For Gold Standard 100% Whey it listed two flavors: Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream.
          </p>

          <p>
            That distinction matters if you are a tested athlete. NSF Certified for Sport means each certified product is screened for substances banned in sport, its label claims are checked and the facility is audited, and the certification applies to the specific product and flavor listed. If you drink Gold Standard in Cookies & Cream or Mocha Cappuccino, the NSF listing for Double Rich Chocolate does not cover your tub. Muscle Milk's Amazon titles also print NSF Certified for Sport directly, which makes it easy to confirm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition's US product page describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested and says its products go through extensive quality testing, but the page does not name a certifier for every flavor. ON has promoted Informed Choice on some products in the past; we could not confirm current Informed Choice listings for each flavor, so we do not claim it here. If certification matters to you, search the <a href="https://www.nsfsport.com/certified-products/">NSF Certified for Sport directory</a> for your exact flavor before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy-metal testing is a separate question. Neither brand publishes batch-level heavy-metal results for these powders on its product pages, and NSF certification focuses on banned substances and label accuracy rather than a public heavy-metal score. Chocolate flavors of any protein tend to carry more lead and cadmium than vanilla because cocoa naturally contains them. If that is a concern, vanilla is the safer default in either brand. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> lists more certified options.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the ready-to-drink shakes, the picture is less clear. The NSF directory we searched still showed older Muscle Milk Genuine shake listings, but the 2026 reformulated cartons are new products, and neither Muscle Milk nor ON states certification for its current shakes on the pages we checked. Treat the shakes as uncertified unless the carton or the NSF directory says otherwise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Is in Optimum Nutrition vs Muscle Milk? Ingredients and Sweeteners
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard has the shorter ingredient list: per the label at the time of writing, Double Rich Chocolate contains a whey protein blend, alkalized cocoa, sunflower and/or soy lecithin, natural and artificial flavor and acesulfame potassium. Muscle Milk Genuine lists around 20 ingredients, including maltodextrin, a non-dairy creamer, canola oil, mixed triglycerides, soluble corn fiber, oat hull fiber, acesulfame potassium, sucralose and added vitamins A, C and D.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners are the first thing most label-readers check. Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate uses acesulfame potassium alone, while some other ON flavors add sucralose, so check your flavor's panel. Both Muscle Milk powders use acesulfame potassium and sucralose together. Neither brand offers a stevia-sweetened version of these powders. If you want to avoid artificial sweeteners entirely, neither tub is your answer; an unflavored whey you sweeten yourself is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens differ slightly. Both brands contain milk, which is unavoidable. Gold Standard may also contain soy from the lecithin, and ON labels list soy as an allergen. The Muscle Milk powders we checked list milk only. Muscle Milk Genuine and Pro Series both include a creamer and oils, which some buyers prefer to avoid but which are not allergens in themselves. If you have a soy allergy, read the Gold Standard label for your flavor; if you have a milk allergy, neither product is suitable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Is Easier on Lactose?</h3>

          <p>
            Gold Standard is likely to be easier for people who are mildly lactose intolerant, because whey isolate, its primary protein, is filtered to remove most lactose. It still contains whey concentrate, so it is not lactose-free. Muscle Milk uses whey and milk protein concentrates, which retain more lactose than isolate, and neither brand prints a lactose figure on these labels. For real lactose intolerance, see our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> or the <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explainer.
          </p>

          <p>
            On taste and mixing, buyers report that Gold Standard mixes easily with a spoon or a few shakes and tastes lighter, and ON itself says it mixes with only a glass and spoon. Buyers describe Muscle Milk Genuine as thicker and creamier, closer to a milkshake, which is the creamer and casein at work. Pro Series draws more mixed reviews, with some buyers finding it thick or chalky. These are buyer reports, not our tasting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Optimum Nutrition vs Muscle Milk Shakes: Which Ready-to-Drink Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Muscle Milk shake is the better ready-to-drink buy: the reformulated carton gives 26 g of protein, 160 calories and 3 g of sugar in 11 fl oz, per its Amazon listing at the time of writing, against 24 g of protein in the Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard carton. At the Amazon prices we saw, the Muscle Milk 12-pack works out to about $1.68 per 25 g of protein and the ON 12-pack to about $2.29.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many people searching this comparison mean the shakes, and the Muscle Milk shake changed a lot in 2026. PepsiCo announced in a <a href="https://www.pepsico.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/muscle-milk-reformulates-protein-shakes-no-artificial-sweeteners-flavors-or-added-colors">May 2026 press release</a> that Muscle Milk shakes moved to ultra-filtered milk with no artificial sweeteners, flavors or added colors. The standard shake rose from about 25 g to 26 g of protein, and the Pro line now runs from 33 g in an 11 fl oz carton to 42 g in a 14 fl oz bottle, as <a href="https://www.foodnavigator.com/Article/2026/06/03/muscle-milk-undergoes-its-biggest-transformation-with-pro-lineup/">FoodNavigator reported</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition's Gold Standard shake takes a different approach. Per ON's product page, it gives 24 g of protein from milk protein isolate, a whey and casein blend, with added zinc and vitamins A, C, D and E, in Chocolate, Vanilla, Strawberry and Café Latte. ON also sells a separate 40 g protein shake with 5 g of fiber. ON does not publish the full nutrition panel as text on its site, so we cannot compare calories and sweeteners line by line; check the carton.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that the shakes reverse the powder story. Gold Standard powder is whey, but Gold Standard shake is milk protein, the same family as Muscle Milk. And Muscle Milk's new cartons are now the cleaner-label product in this pair, while its powders still use sucralose and acesulfame potassium. If a clean sweetener list drew you to this comparison, the Muscle Milk carton is the better pick and the Muscle Milk powder is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            One buying warning: older Muscle Milk Genuine and Pro Series shake listings are still live on Amazon, often from third-party sellers at higher prices. Check that the title says ultra-filtered milk and 26 g, 33 g or 42 g before you buy. For a closer look at the shake market, our <a href="/muscle-milk-vs-premier-protein/">Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein</a> comparison covers the cartons in depth, and <a href="/pure-protein-vs-premier-protein/">Pure Protein vs Premier Protein</a> covers two more popular shakes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is Optimum Nutrition or Muscle Milk Better for Muscle, Weight Loss and Weight Gain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For building muscle, both work, and Pro Series is the best value; for weight loss, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is better; for weight gain, Muscle Milk Genuine is better. Muscle growth depends on total daily protein and training, and every product here supplies a complete milk protein with plenty of leucine. The differences are in what comes along with the protein and what you pay for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard if...</h3>

          <p>
            You want the leanest everyday scoop, you are cutting or maintaining weight, you like having many flavors to choose from, or you prefer a whey-isolate-led protein that digests fast and tends to sit easier with mild lactose sensitivity. It is the default whey for good reason, and our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers the rest of ON's range, including Platinum Hydrowhey and Gold Standard Casein. Tested athletes should stick to the NSF-listed Double Rich Chocolate or Vanilla Ice Cream.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick Muscle Milk if...</h3>

          <p>
            You want the lowest cost per gram of certified protein (Pro Series, ideally used one scoop at a time), you need extra calories to gain weight (Genuine), or you want a creamy, filling shake between meals. Muscle Milk's broad NSF Certified for Sport coverage is also a plus for athletes who like more flavor options with certification. Our <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> covers the 100% Whey and Zero powders too, and you can see Gold Standard against its other big rival in our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-vs-body-fortress/">Optimum Nutrition vs Body Fortress</a> comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a data-only view, our programmatic <a href="/compare/muscle-milk-genuine-protein-powder-vs-optimum-nutrition-gold-standard-whey/">Muscle Milk Genuine vs Gold Standard comparison</a> lines up the two label rows, and the <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide ranks Gold Standard against the rest of the category. A health note: people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone on medication or with a milk allergy should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a high-protein shake every day.
          </p>
        </div>

