              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Both have NSF Certified for Sport listings. When we searched the NSF directory, it listed Muscle Milk Genuine and Pro Series in several flavors each, and Gold Standard 100% Whey in Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream. ON describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested. Certification applies per flavor, so check the directory for the exact tub you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for weight gain, Muscle Milk or Gold Standard?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Muscle Milk Genuine is better for weight gain. Per the label, each serving gives 270 calories, 20 g of carbohydrate and 9 g of fat alongside 32 g of protein, and mixing it with whole milk pushes it past 400 calories. Gold Standard is a lean 120-calorie scoop. For serious bulking, compare Genuine with a dedicated mass gainer, which carries far more calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which shake is better, Muscle Milk or Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The Muscle Milk carton is the better buy. The reformulated 11 fl oz Muscle Milk shake gives 26 g of protein, 160 calories and 3 g of sugar with no artificial sweeteners, per its listing, and cost about $1.68 per 25 g on Amazon. ON's Gold Standard shake gives 24 g of protein and cost about $2.29 per 25 g at the price we saw.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Muscle Milk or Optimum Nutrition have artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Both powders do. Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate uses acesulfame potassium, and some other ON flavors add sucralose. Muscle Milk Genuine and Pro Series use both acesulfame potassium and sucralose, per their labels at the time of writing. The reformulated 2026 Muscle Milk shakes are the exception: Muscle Milk says they contain no artificial sweeteners and use stevia instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
